## Liquidity Event Classification

Hunter evaluates completed candles against persisted liquidity candidates and classifies observable interactions with those levels.

### Event lifecycle

```text
LIQUIDITY CANDIDATE
         |
         v
     VIOLATION
         |
    +----+----+
    |         |
    v         v
 RECLAIM   ACCEPTANCE
    |         |
    v         v
  SWEEP    BREAKOUT
               |
               v
       FAILED BREAKOUT
```

Unresolved violations can also expire when they reach the configured event-age limit.

### Event definitions

| Event | Definition |
|---|---|
| `VIOLATION` | Price trades beyond a candidate liquidity level. |
| `SWEEP` | A violation followed by a close back on the original side within the configured reclaim window. |
| `BREAKOUT` | A violation followed by the configured number of closes beyond the level, establishing acceptance. |
| `FAILED_BREAKOUT` | An accepted breakout subsequently closes back through the liquidity level. |
| `EXPIRED` | An unresolved violation reaches its configured event-age limit. |

A completed candle can contain a complete observable sweep or breakout. Hunter records the observable result without claiming to know the precise sequence of intrabar price movements.

### Event identity and auditability

Event identity includes the symbol, timeframe, liquidity type, side, price level, and violation-candle timestamp. This allows distinct liquidity interactions in the same market to be tracked independently.

The latest event state is stored in `liquidity_events`. Every recorded state transition is retained in `liquidity_event_transitions`.

Persistence is designed to be idempotent: processing the same completed candles again does not duplicate lifecycle transition records.

Hunter distinguishes market observations from subsequent analytical and trading decisions. A liquidity event is not automatically a trade signal.

